# Trivy IaC-scan suppressions for terraform/.
#
# Every entry below is a DELIBERATE, ADR-ratified configuration choice for this
# SHORT-LIVED, SINGLE-OPERATOR EKS *validation* cluster — not an unreviewed risk.
# Each is justified inline and cross-referenced to the ADR that ratifies the
# trade-off. Trivy still FAILS CI on any OTHER CRITICAL/HIGH finding, so this file
# is a triage record, not a blanket mute (see
# .github/workflows/ci.yml § terraform-validate and ADR-019).
#
# Re-triage this list whenever the network / EKS / IAM configuration changes.
# For a persistent or production cluster, the correct action is to REMEDIATE any
# remaining trade-off rather than to keep suppressing it.
#
# RESOLVED (Sprint 7, PR 5 — closes finding M-02): the EKS secrets-encryption
# suppression that used to live here — AVD-AWS-0039 ("EKS should have secrets
# encryption enabled") — has been REMOVED, not re-justified. Kubernetes Secrets are
# now envelope-encrypted with a customer-managed KMS key (terraform/kms.tf +
# aws_eks_cluster.encryption_config in eks.tf); with real CMK encryption configured,
# Trivy no longer raises the finding, so the suppression is gone. See ADR-025 and
# terraform/README.md § Secrets encryption.
#
# RESOLVED (Sprint 7, PR 2 — closes finding H-02): the two EKS API-exposure
# suppressions that used to live here — AVD-AWS-0041 ("open public access CIDRs")
# and AVD-AWS-0040 ("public access enabled") — have been REMOVED, not re-justified.
# The configuration is now secure by default: cluster_endpoint_public_access
# defaults to false (private-only), cluster_endpoint_public_access_cidrs defaults
# to [] and rejects any /0, and preconditions reject "public on with no CIDRs" and
# "both endpoints off". With no default public exposure, Trivy no longer raises
# those findings, so the suppressions are gone. Public access is an explicit,
# CIDR-scoped opt-in that CANNOT be unrestricted. See ADR-022 and
# terraform/eks.tf § Endpoint/security.

# AVD-AWS-0164 (HIGH) — "Subnet associates public IP address."
#   Flags map_public_ip_on_launch = true on the two PUBLIC subnets
#   (aws_subnet.public). This is intentional and correct: public subnets exist to
#   host the NAT gateway and future public load balancers and must auto-assign
#   public IPs by definition. The EKS WORKER NODES — the actual workload — run in
#   the PRIVATE subnets (aws_subnet.private), which leave this unset (false) and
#   have NO public IPs; nodes reach the internet outbound only via NAT. This is
#   the standard EKS public/private VPC split ratified in ADR-015, not exposure of
#   the workload. Remediating would mean rearchitecting PR 2's network — out of
#   scope for a CI-validation PR.
AVD-AWS-0164
